---
import D from '../../data/day2.json';
const R = D.res;
const lb = Object.fromEntries(R.lb.map((r: any[]) => [r[0], r[1]]));
const x3 = (v: number) => v.toFixed(3), x2 = (v: number) => v.toFixed(2), pc = (v: number) => Math.round(100 * v);
const ho = R.l7_heldout;
const fam = Object.fromEntries(R.lb.map((r: any[]) => [r[0], r[4]]));
const bestNN = ['NHITS', 'NHITS_tuned', 'NHITS_noexog', 'NHITSx', 'NHITS_MQ'].reduce((b, m) => lb[m] < lb[b] ? m : b);
const bestSingle = R.lb.filter((r: any[]) => r[4] !== 'ens').reduce((b: any[], r: any[]) => r[1] < b[1] ? r : b)[0];
const day2gain = pc(1 - lb[bestSingle] / lb.LightGBM), nnGain = pc(1 - lb[bestNN] / lb.LightGBM);
const L4 = R.l4, v4 = L4.valid, t4 = L4.test;
const v4best = Object.keys(v4).reduce((b, m) => v4[m][0] < v4[b][0] ? m : b);
const ftGap = v4.Chronos2_ft[0] - v4.Chronos2_zs_cov[0];
const nm4: Record<string, string> = { Chronos2_zs: 'Chronos-2 zero-shot', Chronos2_zs_cov: 'Chronos-2 with covariates', Chronos2_ft: 'fine-tuned Chronos-2', TimesFM_zs: 'TimesFM 2.5', NHITS: 'default NHITS' };
const l1 = R.l1, lgbT = l1.find((r: any[]) => r[0] === 'LightGBM')[1], nnT = l1.filter((r: any[]) => r[0] !== 'LightGBM' && r[0] !== 'DLinear').map((r: any[]) => r[1]);
const nnBest = Math.min(...l1.filter((r: any[]) => r[0] !== 'LightGBM').map((r: any[]) => r[2]));
const loss = R.l2_loss || {}, cost = R.l2_cost;
const beat = R.l2_spread.filter((p: any[]) => p[1] > R.l2_seed).map((p: any[]) => p[0] === 'input_size' ? 'the lookback' : p[0] === 'loss' ? 'the loss' : p[0]);
const tuneX = Math.round(cost[1][1] / cost[0][1]), tuneGain = (100 * (1 - cost[1][2] / cost[0][2])).toFixed(1), noiseX = ((cost[0][2] - cost[1][2]) / R.l2_seed).toFixed(0);
const lift = Object.fromEntries(R.l3_lift);
const [a3, b3] = [R.l3_cov.NHITS_noexog, R.l3_cov.NHITSx];
const l5 = R.l5, olsItem = l5.m['MinT-ols'][4] / l5.base[4], wvItem = l5.m['MinT-wls_var'][4] / l5.base[4], wvTot = l5.m['MinT-wls_var'][0] / l5.base[0];
const T = R.l7_types, win = (t: string) => { const r = T.rows.find((x: any[]) => x[0] === t)[1]; return T.models[r.indexOf(Math.min(...r))]; };
const f = (v: number) => v.toLocaleString('en-US');
const top = R.l7_mean.filter((r: any[]) => !/^(ETS|AutoETS|Naive|SeasonalNaive|DLinear)/.test(r[0])).slice(0, 10).map((r: any[]) => r[0]);
const btO = R.l7_bt.map((r: any[]) => r[0]).filter((m: string) => top.includes(m));
const mover = top.slice(0, 6).reduce((b: string, m: string) => (top.indexOf(m) - btO.indexOf(m)) > (top.indexOf(b) - btO.indexOf(b)) ? m : b);
const ord = (n: number) => n + (['th', 'st', 'nd', 'rd'][n % 10 > 3 || [11, 12, 13].includes(n % 100) ? 0 : n % 10]);
---
<section class="d2 part">
  <div class="plate">
    <h3>The final leaderboard</h3>
    <div class="ctrl"><div class="seg" id="lbMetric"><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="mean">Mean RMSSE</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="median">Median RMSSE</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="wape">WAPE</button></div><label><input type="checkbox" id="lbEns" checked> show ensembles (optimistic)</label></div>
    <div class="fig" id="figLB"></div>
    <div class="legend" id="lbLegend"></div>
    <p class="take">Ensemble rows here include the half of the series that chose them, so they look better than they are. On the held-out half the top-3 average scores {x3(ho.top3)} vs {x3(ho.best_single[1])} for the best single model ({ho.best_single[0]}) and {x3(ho.lightgbm)} for Day 1's LightGBM.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>Every metric, every model</h3>
    <div class="ctrl"><div class="seg" id="suiteGroup"><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="scaled">Scaled</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="rel">vs SeasonalNaive</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="pct">Percentage</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="vol">Volume</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="bias">Bias</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="type">By demand type</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="int">Intervals</button></div></div>
    <div class="tw" id="suiteTable"></div>
    <p class="cap" id="suiteNote"></p>
    <p class="cap">The workshop's metric suite on the test weeks, from <code>utilsforecast</code> and <code>sktime</code> (none hand-written). Click a column to sort; green marks the best single model (ensembles, marked *, are left out of that). The labs save every forecast, so any other metric can be computed later without refitting.</p>
    <p class="take">The ranking depends on the question. {(() => { const S = R.suite, single = Object.keys(S).filter(m => fam[m] !== 'ens'); const by = (k: string) => single.reduce((b, m) => S[m][k] < S[b][k] ? m : b); return `${by('mean_rmsse')} has the lowest mean RMSSE, ${by('median_rmsse')} the lowest median, ${by('pooled_wape')} the lowest volume-weighted WAPE and ${by('mean_mase')} the lowest mean MASE`; })()}. On bias, LightGBM over-forecasts total volume by about {pc(R.lb_bias.LightGBM)} %; the two Chronos-2 models and NHITSx stay within {Math.ceil(100 * Math.max(...['NHITSx', 'Chronos2_ft', 'Chronos2_zs_cov'].map(m => Math.abs(R.lb_bias[m]))))} %.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>The day as a journey (mean RMSSE)</h3>
    <div class="fig" id="figJourney"></div>
    <p class="take">The biggest single step is Day 1's: going global with covariates ({x3(lb.SeasonalNaive)} → {x3(lb.LightGBM)}). Day 2's best single model ({bestSingle}) adds about {day2gain} % more; the best model trained only on our data ({bestNN}) adds about {nnGain} %. Averaging adds a little on top.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="plate">
      <h3>Lab 01 · accuracy vs training time</h3>
      <div class="fig" id="figL1"></div>
      <p class="take">{nnBest > lb.LightGBM ? 'Out of the box no neural model beats LightGBM' : 'Out of the box the best neural model edges LightGBM'}, and they take {Math.round(Math.min(...nnT) / lgbT)}–{Math.round(Math.max(...nnT) / lgbT)}× longer to fit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="plate">
      <h3>Lab 02 · what tuning buys</h3>
      <div class="fig" id="figL2"></div>
      <p class="take">Only {beat.join(' and ')} move the score by more than the seed noise ({R.l2_seed.toFixed(3)}){loss.MSE && loss.MAE ? `; the loss most (validation RMSSE ${x2(loss.MSE)} with MSE vs ${x2(loss.MAE)} with MAE)` : ''}. Eight Optuna trials cost {tuneX}× the compute of one default fit for a {tuneGain} % gain on the test window.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="plate">
      <h3>Lab 03 · do the models respond to a promotion?</h3>
      <div class="fig" id="figL3"></div>
      <p class="take">Direction right, size timid. The raw +{pc(lift.raw)} % is an upper bound: promotions coincide with season and price cuts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="plate">
      <h3>Lab 03 · covariates: mean vs typical series</h3>
      <div class="fig" id="figL3b"></div>
      <p class="take">{b3[0] < a3[0] ? `NHITSx wins the mean too, but only just (${x3(b3[0])} vs ${x3(a3[0])})` : `NHITS without covariates wins the mean (${x3(a3[0])} vs ${x3(b3[0])})`}; NHITSx is better on {pc(R.l3_better)} % of series, on the median and on WAPE. Intermittent items hold the mean back.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="plate" id="lab04">
    <h3>Lab 04 · foundation models: fine-tune or not?</h3>
    <div class="fig" id="figL4"></div>
    <p class="cap">All {f(R.n_series)} series. Lab 04 decides on the validation weeks and scores the test weeks once. Chronos-2 and TimesFM 2.5 run through <code>timecopilot</code>; SeasonalNaive and NHITS are the same-day yardsticks. Seconds are for validation on a 4-core CPU without a GPU.</p>
    <p class="take">{nm4[v4best]} is the best model on the validation weeks ({x3(v4[v4best][0])}); fine-tuning made validation worse by {ftGap.toFixed(3)}, so the lab's rule keeps the zero-shot model. On the test weeks the fine-tuned model edges ahead ({x3(t4.Chronos2_ft[0])} vs {x3(t4.Chronos2_zs_cov[0])}), a gap smaller than the seed noise Lab 02 measured for NHITS ({R.l2_seed.toFixed(3)}). Both beat {bestNN} ({x3(lb[bestNN])}), the best model trained only on our data. Without price and promotions, Chronos-2 ({x3(t4.Chronos2_zs[0])}) and TimesFM ({x3(t4.TimesFM_zs[0])}) land near {nm4.NHITS} ({x3(t4.NHITS[0])}): the gain comes from pretraining plus the covariates, not from pretraining alone.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>Lab 05 · reconciliation: gain or loss at each level</h3>
    <div class="ctrl"><div class="seg" id="l5Toggle"></div></div>
    <div class="fig" id="figL5"></div>
    <p class="take">Ratio to the incoherent base forecasts (below 1 = better). Bars are cut off at 3× for readability; MinT-ols is {olsItem.toFixed(1)}× worse at the items. MinT-wls_var matches the base at the items ({x2(wvItem)}×) and is coherent, at a cost of {pc(wvTot - 1)} % on the store total.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="plate">
      <h3>Lab 06 · do the intervals cover what they promise?</h3>
      <div class="fig" id="figL6"></div>
      <p class="take">Only quantile loss reaches the target; pooled conformal comes close. Per-series conformal is capped by n = 3.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="plate">
      <h3>Lab 07 · who wins where (mean RMSSE by demand type)</h3>
      <div class="fig" id="figL7"></div>
      <p class="take">No model wins everywhere: {['smooth', 'erratic', 'lumpy', 'intermittent'].map(t => `${win(t)} takes ${t}`).join(', ')}. The foundation models win the items that sell most weeks; on intermittent items a neural model without covariates is still best. These means use the {f(T.rows.reduce((z: number, r: any[]) => z + r[2], 0))} series every model forecast.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>Lab 07 · two rankings: mean RMSSE vs head-to-head rating</h3>
    <div class="fig" id="figL7b"></div>
    <p class="take">Lines that cross are models the mean and the duels disagree on. {mover} is {ord(top.indexOf(mover) + 1)} on the mean but {ord(btO.indexOf(mover) + 1)} in the duels: it wins many ordinary series by a little and loses some intermittent ones by a lot. At the top, {btO[0]} leads the duels and {top[0]} the mean.</p>
  </div>
</section>
